WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.612
Model weights, visual components, context buffers, image tokens, and runtime overhead all consume memory.

00:00:06.612 --> 00:00:14.076
Available VRAM or unified memory often determines which model and quantization can run without heavy offloading.

00:00:14.076 --> 00:00:19.835
System RAM still matters when weights or caches spill beyond accelerator memory.

00:00:19.835 --> 00:00:24.314
NVIDIA GPUs have broad local-inference support across many runtimes.

00:00:24.314 --> 00:00:29.646
AMD support depends more on operating system, backend, and model format.

00:00:29.646 --> 00:00:42.655
Apple Silicon uses unified memory shared by CPU and GPU, which can make larger models practical but also means the operating system and applications compete for the same pool.

00:00:42.655 --> 00:00:50.120
CPU inference is widely available but may be too slow for larger models or frequent images.

00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:55.879
A GPU or accelerator improves throughput when the runtime supports it correctly.

00:00:55.879 --> 00:01:02.490
Measure actual utilization; unsupported operations can fall back to the CPU and change performance.

00:01:02.490 --> 00:01:13.794
Hardware tiers such as 4 to 8 gigabytes, 12 to 16 gigabytes, and 24 gigabytes or more are useful planning categories, not permanent model recommendations.

00:01:13.794 --> 00:01:17.846
Smaller tiers favor compact models and lower quantization.

00:01:17.846 --> 00:01:22.325
Larger tiers support more capacity, but still need headroom.

00:01:22.325 --> 00:01:29.790
Larger models can improve some tasks while increasing latency, power, and failure risk under memory pressure.

00:01:29.790 --> 00:01:35.548
Concurrency and image size can matter more than a single prompt benchmark.

00:01:35.548 --> 00:01:43.013
Shortlist configurations that fit comfortably, then test speed, memory, temperature, power, and completion under representative load.

00:01:43.013 --> 00:01:50.478
The right size is the smallest configuration that meets quality and reliability needs with operational headroom.
